{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And now</b> — The Gaon [Saadia] said: the kings of Media and Persia are four, and they are: Darius the Mede, and Cyrus the Persian, also Ahasuerus, and Darius the Persian, whom Alexander killed. And he explained \"behold, yet three kings\" together with Cyrus, and the meaning of \"and the fourth,\" who is Darius the Persian, is that he is fourth to Darius the Mede, for this prophecy was in the third year of Cyrus the Persian. And R. Moses the Cohen the Sephardi said: there were six kings: the first Darius the Elder, who is the Mede; the second Cyrus the Persian; the third Ahasuerus; the fourth Artaxerxes, who ordered that the holy city not be rebuilt; the fifth Darius the Persian, who ordered the House to be built; and the sixth is Artaxerxes the rich, whom Alexander killed — he is the one under whom Ezra and also Nehemiah went up, as is written. And in my opinion: the meaning of \"behold, yet three kings\" is that the fourth is not counted with them, for the verse says \"yet\" that they are yet to come, and one may challenge this: if the meaning of \"yet\" is thus, why did he say \"behold, yet three kings,\" for his wording implies no more, and why did he say \"and the fourth\" — and behold, it would have been fitting to say \"behold, yet four kings\"?! And there is further room to object: behold, Cyrus reigned three years, as is written, and it is written in the Scroll that the affair of Haman was in the twelfth year of the kingdom of Ahasuerus, and in the Book of the Kings of Persia that his entire reign was fourteen years — if so, when did the first Artaxerxes, who ordered that the House not be built, reign?! And in my opinion: there are five kings of Media and Persia: the first Darius the Mede, the second Cyrus the Persian, the third is Ahasuerus — and this name is in the language of the Persians, and in the Chaldean tongue it is Artaxerxes — he is the one who ordered that Jerusalem not be rebuilt, and thus it is written: \"in the days of Ahasuerus, at the beginning of his reign, they wrote slander against the inhabitants of Judah and Jerusalem\" — and behold, the interpretation of the slander and the letter is in the language of the Targum as it was written, and the interpretation of the passage mentioned \"in the days of Artaxerxes\" was written in peace, and this slander is not the words of Haman, for it was not at the beginning of his reign; rather, when Ahasuerus reigned he ordered that the Jews go up and build the House, therefore it is written \"for it went up from beside him,\" for they went with his permission, and because of the letter written to him he turned sour like Cyrus, and this is \"by the decree of Cyrus and Darius and Artaxerxes king of Persia.\" And according to the plain sense, if he had been one king, it would have been written \"Cyrus, he is Darius, he is Artaxerxes,\" according to the rule of the language. And behold, after Cyrus there were three alone: Ahasuerus, who is the first Artaxerxes; the second Darius the Persian, who is fourth to Cyrus, in whose days the prophecy was; he is Artaxerxes, who reigned many years, for behold it is written in Ezra, the thirtieth year of his kingdom, and there it is written that he reigned in Babylon, for thus the Babylonians called his name Artaxerxes, and his name in the language of Persia was Darius, for it is written in the Book of the Kings of Persia that Darius was the name of the king whom Alexander killed." "<b>And the meaning of \"he shall stir up all\"</b> — for the men of his kingdom sent for Alexander, and his princes also killed him at night in the place where he fled. This is Ahasuerus, who was Darius."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "Resolved הגאון as the Gaon [Saadia] by standard convention. The argument is dense and compressed; preserved as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:21:21.868255808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}